Parent governance: #19 / merged PR #20.
Architecture target: #22 / merged PR #23 / `SDP/Architecture/AS-#22/APS--001.md`.

Review target product baseline: `v0.3.14` / `f626077ca0e59fbe9ede7ba1116982bb94b2eb6b` (governance/architecture-documentation commits after this SHA are not product-code changes).

Role: Broad Reviewer.

Read completely before starting:
- root `AGENTS.md`;
- `developmentInstructions.md`;
- this Issue #21;
- `SDP/Architecture/AS-#22/APS--001.md`;
- `SDP/Templates/BroadReview.md`.

Purpose:
Review the whole current repository at system/architecture level against the accepted Architecture Pre-study target hypothesis. Do NOT implement fixes. Do NOT spend the session doing deep line-by-line review of one horizontal layer; that belongs in later focused Layer Reviews.

A central objective is to determine how current production code must be redistributed into explicit horizontal design layers with separate physical directories/packages and clear contracts between them. The Broad Review must not merely name conceptual layers while leaving vertically mixed implementation files in place.

Target architecture hypothesis from APS--001:
- Domain
- Application
- Git
- GitHub
- Runtime
- Launch Discovery
- Persistence
- TUI State
- TUI View
- Composition

The reviewer must challenge these names/boundaries with code evidence; they are a target hypothesis, not frozen refactor design.

Required Broad Review goals:
- map current de-facto packages/files to the target logical layers;
- identify production files/packages that mix responsibilities from multiple target layers;
- identify which files should be split, moved, removed or replaced to achieve physical layer separation;
- identify shared/legacy files that cannot safely be assigned to parallel workers and therefore need Master ownership during migration;
- identify vertical Slice failures and map their likely horizontal/boundary causes;
- identify duplicated/ambiguous state authority and async event ordering problems;
- identify process/session/resource lifecycle ownership problems;
- identify Git/worktree/domain-state boundaries and local-vs-upstream modeling problems;
- identify TUI interaction-state versus rendering boundaries, including the stacked V310..V314 wrapper architecture;
- identify launch-discovery versus process/runtime ownership leaks;
- identify missing/incorrect interfaces and dependency direction between layers;
- compare the APS candidate BC set with current implementation evidence and propose additions/revisions;
- propose the set and order of focused per-layer reviews;
- assess migration order so multiple layer workers can operate on disjoint physical folders/branches with minimal merge conflict;
- recommend whether the refactor should use the current minor line or open a new architecture line. APS--001 preliminarily recommends `v0.4`; Broad Review must independently confirm or reject that recommendation.

Known symptoms to challenge independently, not accept as conclusions:
- running launch scripts may not stop reliably, especially process trees on Windows;
- async console-start/restart completion may steal focus after the user navigates away;
- interactive shell process existence may be confused with active work;
- local branch tip vs upstream/GitHub tip is not modeled/presented explicitly enough;
- historical TUI wrapper layers may have overlapping state ownership.

Architecture-specific questions the review must address:
- Which current `worktree`, `graph`, and `diff` responsibilities belong in Git versus Application?
- Which current `app.Service` responsibilities are real orchestration versus concrete-adapter facade behavior?
- How should the current `tui` package be split into TUI State and TUI View without carrying cross-layer orchestration into either?
- How should `launch` split into Launch Discovery and Runtime/session ownership?
- What Domain types can become pure and stable enough to be shared without leaking adapters upward?
- What Application ports/interfaces are required so concrete adapters do not import/call each other?
- Which dependency rules should eventually be enforced in CI?

Deliverable:
`SDP/Reviews/CR-#21/Broad/BR--001.md`
using `SDP/Templates/BroadReview.md`.

The report must end with:
- finding IDs and severities;
- current de-facto layer map;
- current-to-target migration map;
- explicit list of mixed-responsibility files/packages and proposed target owners;
- required focused Layer Review issues in proposed execution order;
- preliminary BC changes/candidates;
- parallel-migration ownership/conflict-risk notes;
- release-line recommendation;
- exact next permitted action.

No refactor implementation begins from this Issue. After the Broad Review is accepted, create/execute the focused Layer Review issues first.

